      "seed": 582336506,
      "version": 1292,
      "versionNonce": 2053242362,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784134940294,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- this approach is not feasible as the query takes lot of time unnecessarily increasing load on DB, and to achieve near real time we need to query every few ms\n\n- To view history of comments to achieve infinite scrolling, what we need is N most recent comments that occurred before a certain timestamp\n- Introduce pagination, this loads up new result every time user scrolls more\n    - Poor Choice - Offset Pagination : uses an offset to specify the starting point for fetching a set of result.\n                        GET /comments/:liveVideoId?offset=0&pagesize=10\n                        inefficient as volume grows, DB must count through all rows preceding the offset for each query, not stable, if a comment is added or deleted while user scrolling, offset\n                        gives incorrect and user sees missing or duplicates comments.\n    - Great Choice : Cursor Pagination : Uses a cursor to specify the start, which is a pointer that points to specific item in a list, initially set to most recent comment\n                        - We built an index on the cursor field so need not to go through all the rows, is stable, handles correctly displaying addition/deletion of comment\n                        GET /comments/:liveVideoId?cursor={last_comment_id}&pageSize=10",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- this approach is not feasible as the query takes lot of time unnecessarily increasing load on DB, and to achieve near real time we need to query every few ms\n\n- To view history of comments to achieve infinite scrolling, what we need is N most recent comments that occurred before a certain timestamp\n- Introduce pagination, this loads up new result every time user scrolls more\n    - Poor Choice - Offset Pagination : uses an offset to specify the starting point for fetching a set of result.\n                        GET /comments/:liveVideoId?offset=0&pagesize=10\n                        inefficient as volume grows, DB must count through all rows preceding the offset for each query, not stable, if a comment is added or deleted while user scrolling, offset\n                        gives incorrect and user sees missing or duplicates comments.\n    - Great Choice : Cursor Pagination : Uses a cursor to specify the start, which is a pointer that points to specific item in a list, initially set to most recent comment\n                        - We built an index on the cursor field so need not to go through all the rows, is stable, handles correctly displaying addition/deletion of comment\n                        GET /comments/:liveVideoId?cursor={last_comment_id}&pageSize=10",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
