      "updated": 1784142077691,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "2. Great Solution : SSE\n- simpler than websockets, unidirectional\nProblem : some proxies/LB lack support for streaming responses, leading to buffering issues\n- Browsers impose limits on concurrent SSE connections per domain, creating problems for users to watch multiple live videos simultaneously.\n",
      "fontSize": 20.10031680045577,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2. Great Solution : SSE\n- simpler than websockets, unidirectional\nProblem : some proxies/LB lack support for streaming responses, leading to buffering issues\n- Browsers impose limits on concurrent SSE connections per domain, creating problems for users to watch multiple live videos simultaneously.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "do1ex0Y8N6OtyyLToHFVv",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -41271.969244854845,
      "y": -5099.733319523105,
      "width": 2451.118896484375,
      "height": 975,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aD",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1293117818,
      "version": 3882,
      "versionNonce": 730639674,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784144533903,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to scale to million of concurrent viewers?\n- with so many concurrent viewers we need to scale our primary server\n- But say comment landed on server1 and viewers are watching through many servers, so how does server1 knows what all server to notify so that clients connected through SSE to those servers gets notified?\n\nNote : connection capacity is not limited to 65535, this number refers to range of port nos not the number of connections a single server port can handle\nEach TCP connection is identified by a unique combination of source IP, source port, destination IP and destination port, with proper OS tuning, a single listening port can handle hundreds of thousands or even millions of concurrent SSE connections.\n\n\n1. Good Solution : Naive Pub/Sub: Every server processes every comment\n- first separate out read service and write service because the traffic is so different, so create Realtime Messaging Servers responsible to send comments to viewers.\n- now client through a LB evenly connect to many instance of this Realtime Messaging Server, informing servier which live video they are watching and server maintains a mapping in local memory\n    {\n        \"liveVideoId1\": [\"sseConnection1\", \"sseConnection2\"],\n        \"liveVideoId2\": [\"sseConnection3\", \"sseConnection4\"]\n    }\n- When new comment created, comment management service publishes message to a channel and all realtime servers subscribe to this and then send the comment to all viewers connected to them.\n\nProblem : works but inefficient at scale, each realtime server processes every comment across all live videos even if none of its connected viewers are watching that video, wasted compute\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Partitioned Pub/Sub with Viewer Co-location\n- Improve by partitioning comment stream into different channels, each realtime server subscribes to only the channels it needs, determined by viewers connected to it.\n- But having a channel per live video would consume lot of resources, infeasible with Kafka, we use a hashing function to distribute across N channels hash(liveVideoId) % N\n- But with round-robin LB, a server could endup with viewers subscribed to many different streams forcing it to subscribe to many channels \n- Solution is intelligent routing, L7 LB use CH on liveVideoId to route viewers of same video to same server\n- It inspects the request header, NGNIX or Envoy ensuring related viewers stay together.\n- Or store dynamic mapping of liveVideoId to server in a coordination service Zookeeper\nProblem : managing coordination b/w LB and pub/sub i.e. if server's viewers changes it needs to update its subscriptions accordingly\n\n\n\n3. Great Solution : Dispatcher Service Instead of Pub/Sub\n- Introduce a Dispatcher Service that directly routes each new comment to correct Realtime Server\n- Dispatcher maintains a dynamic map of servers responsible for which videos, kept in sync via heartbeats and forwards comments directly to those servers.\n- Eliminates the need of pub/sub, centralised routing logic \nProblem : keeping dispatcher map accurate during rapid changes, during viral streams dispatcher needs frequent refreshes, and if multiple dispatcher instances then it needs a coordinated cache invalidation\n\nNote : Kafka is highly scalable but struggles with dynamic subscription patterns like switching b/w videos. Redis pub/sub provides low latency, handles dynamic subscription well\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to scale to million of concurrent viewers?\n- with so many concurrent viewers we need to scale our primary server\n- But say comment landed on server1 and viewers are watching through many servers, so how does server1 knows what all server to notify so that clients connected through SSE to those servers gets notified?\n\nNote : connection capacity is not limited to 65535, this number refers to range of port nos not the number of connections a single server port can handle\nEach TCP connection is identified by a unique combination of source IP, source port, destination IP and destination port, with proper OS tuning, a single listening port can handle hundreds of thousands or even millions of concurrent SSE connections.\n\n\n1. Good Solution : Naive Pub/Sub: Every server processes every comment\n- first separate out read service and write service because the traffic is so different, so create Realtime Messaging Servers responsible to send comments to viewers.\n- now client through a LB evenly connect to many instance of this Realtime Messaging Server, informing servier which live video they are watching and server maintains a mapping in local memory\n    {\n        \"liveVideoId1\": [\"sseConnection1\", \"sseConnection2\"],\n        \"liveVideoId2\": [\"sseConnection3\", \"sseConnection4\"]\n    }\n- When new comment created, comment management service publishes message to a channel and all realtime servers subscribe to this and then send the comment to all viewers connected to them.\n\nProblem : works but inefficient at scale, each realtime server processes every comment across all live videos even if none of its connected viewers are watching that video, wasted compute\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Partitioned Pub/Sub with Viewer Co-location\n- Improve by partitioning comment stream into different channels, each realtime server subscribes to only the channels it needs, determined by viewers connected to it.\n- But having a channel per live video would consume lot of resources, infeasible with Kafka, we use a hashing function to distribute across N channels hash(liveVideoId) % N\n- But with round-robin LB, a server could endup with viewers subscribed to many different streams forcing it to subscribe to many channels \n- Solution is intelligent routing, L7 LB use CH on liveVideoId to route viewers of same video to same server\n- It inspects the request header, NGNIX or Envoy ensuring related viewers stay together.\n- Or store dynamic mapping of liveVideoId to server in a coordination service Zookeeper\nProblem : managing coordination b/w LB and pub/sub i.e. if server's viewers changes it needs to update its subscriptions accordingly\n\n\n\n3. Great Solution : Dispatcher Service Instead of Pub/Sub\n- Introduce a Dispatcher Service that directly routes each new comment to correct Realtime Server\n- Dispatcher maintains a dynamic map of servers responsible for which videos, kept in sync via heartbeats and forwards comments directly to those servers.\n- Eliminates the need of pub/sub, centralised routing logic \nProblem : keeping dispatcher map accurate during rapid changes, during viral streams dispatcher needs frequent refreshes, and if multiple dispatcher instances then it needs a coordinated cache invalidation\n\nNote : Kafka is highly scalable but struggles with dynamic subscription patterns like switching b/w videos. Redis pub/sub provides low latency, handles dynamic subscription well\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "TWcXrBpd9hMgtve3ouA5c",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -41264.66454352214,
      "y": -2228.254868469236,
      "width": 2085.11865234375,
      "height": 550,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aE",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1064832294,
      "version": 2409,
      "versionNonce": 1585573498,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784146865420,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Mega Streams\n- during world cup finals 5000 comments/sec, that's 1 comment needs to be pushed off in 4 ms i.e. gets a screen time such low, not possible for human to read and us to scale\n- On such scale, they need to have a \"vibe\" of collective participation rather than reading conversation\n\n1. Good Solution : Show a representative Subset\n- sample the comment stream and only deliver a subset to each viewer\n- maintain a sampling rate based on comments arriving velocity, say if 100/s then sample 50% at 5000/s only sample 1-2%\n- the allows a readable experience\n- Can add rules on the comments to sample say prioritize comments getting reactions or comments from verified accounts or comments from account that users follow, ensuring best comment gets seen by everyone\n\nProblems : We're still maintaining persistent SSE to millions of concurrent viewers and pushing updates in real-time, infra costs high\n\n\n2. Great Solution : CDN-Based Delivery with Periodic Snapshots\n- On server side, maintain a ring buffer of most recent 100-200 comments for each mega-stream\n- Every second, take a snapshot of this buffer and write to redis or directly to CDN origin, The CDC distributes it globally, caching it at edge locatoins\n- On client side, don't maintain SSE connections now, instead the app polls the CDN every second for latest snapshot\n- Upon getting the snapshot the client doesn't dump all comments on screen that's not readable instead it timely space it out to simulate a continuous stream\n- Leverages the existing CDN network used to deliver the content(Live Video)\n- So when some threshold reaches(100,000 viewers or 500 comments/sec) auto flip to CDN from SSE\n- \"Read your own write\" i.e. my comment should be visible to me immediately is handled at client layer itself\nProblem : transitioning back to SSE when viewers drop is complex, bit of latency but meaning less for mega streams",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Mega Streams\n- during world cup finals 5000 comments/sec, that's 1 comment needs to be pushed off in 4 ms i.e. gets a screen time such low, not possible for human to read and us to scale\n- On such scale, they need to have a \"vibe\" of collective participation rather than reading conversation\n\n1. Good Solution : Show a representative Subset\n- sample the comment stream and only deliver a subset to each viewer\n- maintain a sampling rate based on comments arriving velocity, say if 100/s then sample 50% at 5000/s only sample 1-2%\n- the allows a readable experience\n- Can add rules on the comments to sample say prioritize comments getting reactions or comments from verified accounts or comments from account that users follow, ensuring best comment gets seen by everyone\n\nProblems : We're still maintaining persistent SSE to millions of concurrent viewers and pushing updates in real-time, infra costs high\n\n\n2. Great Solution : CDN-Based Delivery with Periodic Snapshots\n- On server side, maintain a ring buffer of most recent 100-200 comments for each mega-stream\n- Every second, take a snapshot of this buffer and write to redis or directly to CDN origin, The CDC distributes it globally, caching it at edge locatoins\n- On client side, don't maintain SSE connections now, instead the app polls the CDN every second for latest snapshot\n- Upon getting the snapshot the client doesn't dump all comments on screen that's not readable instead it timely space it out to simulate a continuous stream\n- Leverages the existing CDN network used to deliver the content(Live Video)\n- So when some threshold reaches(100,000 viewers or 500 comments/sec) auto flip to CDN from SSE\n- \"Read your own write\" i.e. my comment should be visible to me immediately is handled at client layer itself\nProblem : transitioning back to SSE when viewers drop is complex, bit of latency but meaning less for mega streams",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "q8uWC04VBGorLVvxYT7eU",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -41259.648703499224,
      "y": -1565.1608174561306,
      "width": 1633.0390625,
      "height": 325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aF",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1645222,
      "version": 1333,
      "versionNonce": 66195258,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784146865420,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to handle client disconnects and they don't miss comments?\n- with flaky networks we don't want to loose on comments\n- In case of disconnect we can't ignore it, the comments while disconnected are lost creating poor user experience\n\n- Last-Event-ID with Client Side Tracking\n    - SSE has built in mechanism for handling reconnections using Last-Event-ID header\n    - using this lastID replay or resend the missed comments upon reconnection before resuming normal stream\n    - Client also tracks lastCommentId locally and requests catchup, this build better user experience around reconnection\n    - Client animates missed comments on 2-3x speed or says \"You missed 47 comments\" and an option to jump live\n    - set limit on how farm back you're gonna go, can't show 1hr comments if disconnected for 1H\n\nProblem : If reconnected to a different realtime server, it would need access to comments history, solved using a shared Redis cache to replay comments for any video\ncoordination b/w SSE stream and HTTP catch-up is tough could cause gaps or duplicates",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to handle client disconnects and they don't miss comments?\n- with flaky networks we don't want to loose on comments\n- In case of disconnect we can't ignore it, the comments while disconnected are lost creating poor user experience\n\n- Last-Event-ID with Client Side Tracking\n    - SSE has built in mechanism for handling reconnections using Last-Event-ID header\n    - using this lastID replay or resend the missed comments upon reconnection before resuming normal stream\n    - Client also tracks lastCommentId locally and requests catchup, this build better user experience around reconnection\n    - Client animates missed comments on 2-3x speed or says \"You missed 47 comments\" and an option to jump live\n    - set limit on how farm back you're gonna go, can't show 1hr comments if disconnected for 1H\n\nProblem : If reconnected to a different realtime server, it would need access to comments history, solved using a shared Redis cache to replay comments for any video\ncoordination b/w SSE stream and HTTP catch-up is tough could cause gaps or duplicates",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
